A tier>=3 VENDORED library_export could still be the top `slice --entry
auto` / `--reverse` seed, so a slice of a Django app started from a
bundled pdf.worker.js instead of a view. Two structural causes, neither
a tuning threshold:

1. The library_export demotion is gated on the entry's own language
   having semantic entrypoints -- a carve-out built to protect
   FIRST-PARTY API surface (a Lean library's exports must not be
   suppressed by incidental Python helper scripts). Vendored third-party
   code is never the analyzed repo's API surface in any language, so the
   carve-out no longer extends to tier>=3. Scoped by supply-chain tier,
   not by a second hand-curated vendor-path list.

2. The demotion alone was NOT enough -- the half the filed
   "0.75 * 0.3 = 0.225 > the 0.10 floor" framing missed. The
   connectivity boost is ADDITIVE, so x0.1 -> 0.0225 plus an in-degree
   boost of up to +0.35 climbs back over the ranking floor. The codebase
   had already learned this twice (infra_export_ids, build_wrapper_ids),
   so all three now share one `boost_suppressed_ids` set and one skip
   check instead of a third parallel set.

Behavioral evidence (zellij, real repo, identical nodes/edges -- only
the ranking function differs): the two vendored termwiz exports go from
surviving at rank 0.60 / 0.57 to filtered out entirely, other top seeds
unchanged.

The reverse slice resolves `--entry auto` through the same
detect_entrypoints call and is covered by the same fix. The item's
proposed blanket "constrain reverse-slice seeds to tier 1" is
deliberately NOT implemented: tier 2 is internal_dep, genuinely
first-party monorepo code and a legitimate seed, and --max-tier already
exists as the explicit user knob.

